# In C, the loader is a critical OS component that acts right at runtime. When you run an executable, 
the loader reads the file from disk, allocates memory, maps virtual memory segments, links shared libraries, 
initializes the stack and CPU registers, and transfers execution control to the main program.

# Type casting in C is the process of converting a variable or value from one data type to another. 
It allows you to temporarily alter how the compiler interprets a piece of data to perform specific operations, maintain precision, or ensure compatibility across different data types.

Key Differences at a Glance

Feature: Done By
Implicit Conversion: Performed by the compiler
Explicit Type Casting: Initiated manually by the programmer

Feature: Operator
Implicit Conversion: No operator is needed
Explicit Type Casting: Requires the (type) cast operator

Feature: Data Loss
Implicit Conversion: Generally safe; upgrades types
Explicit Type Casting: Risk of data loss if downgrading types

Feature: Direction
Implicit Conversion: Narrowing to widening (e.g., int to float)
Explicit Type Casting: Any direction (e.g., float to int truncates decimals)

