1. The Program (Question) :

#include <stdio.h>
void main()
{
    int a = 10, b = 8, c = 7, d = 6, e, f, g, h;

    e = (a > 2) && (c != d);
    f = (a != b) || (c > d);

    g = e++;
    h = ++g;

    printf("%d %d %d %d %d %d %d %d",
           a++, ++b, c++, ++d, e++, ++f, g++, ++h);
}

2. Process (Explanation) :

Step 1: Initial values
a=10, b=8, c=7, d=6

Step 2: Evaluate logical expressions
e = (a > 2) && (c != d)
  = (10 > 2) && (7 != 6)
  = 1 && 1
  = 1

f = (a != b) || (c > d)
  = (10 != 8) || (7 > 6)
  = 1 || 1   → short-circuits at first true condition
  = 1

Step 3: Increment operations on e, g, h
g = e++   → g takes e's current value (1), THEN e increases by 1
  → g = 1, e becomes 2

h = ++g   → g increases by 1 FIRST, then h takes that value
  → g = 2, h = 2

Values just before the printf line:
a=10, b=8, c=7, d=6, e=2, f=1, g=2, h=2

Step 4: Evaluate printf arguments left to right
a++   → prints old value 10, then a becomes 11
++b   → b becomes 9,  prints 9
c++   → prints old value 7,  then c becomes 8
++d   → d becomes 7,  prints 7
e++   → prints old value 2,  then e becomes 3
++f   → f becomes 2,  prints 2
g++   → prints old value 2,  then g becomes 3
++h   → h becomes 3,  prints 3

Key rule:
- Post-increment (x++) → USE the current value first, increase AFTER
- Pre-increment (++x)  → INCREASE first, THEN use the new value

